"Internal Error ()\nciteturn5954view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09536v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09536v1: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09539v1] SynClaimEval: A Framework for Evaluating the Utility of Synthetic Data in Long-Context Claim Verification (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09539v1)\nciteturn5954view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09539v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:SynClaimEval: A Framework for Evaluating the Utility of Synthetic Data in Long-Context Claim Verification\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Mohamed Elaraby , cite6†Jyoti Prakash Maheswari L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SynClaimEval: A Framework for Evaluating the Utility of Synthetic Data in Long-Context Claim Verification, by Mohamed Elaraby and Jyoti Prakash Maheswari\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) with extended context windows promise direct reasoning over long documents, reducing the need for chunking or retrieval. Constructing annotated resources for training and evaluation, however, remains costly. Synthetic data offers a scalable alternative, and we introduce SynClaimEval, a framework for evaluating synthetic data utility in long-context claim verification -- a task central to hallucination detection and fact-checking.\nL17: Our framework examines three dimensions: (i) input characteristics, by varying context length and testing generalization to out-of-domain benchmarks; (ii) synthesis logic, by controlling claim complexity and error type variation; and (iii) explanation quality, measuring the degree to which model explanations provide evidence consistent with predictions.\nL18: Experiments across benchmarks show that long-context synthesis can improve verification in base instruction-tuned models, particularly when augmenting existing human-written datasets. Moreover, synthesis enhances explanation quality, even when verification scores do not improve, underscoring its potential to strengthen both performance and explainability.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2511.09539 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2511.09539v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09539†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | The 5th Workshop on Evaluation & Comparison of NLP Systems, 2025\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Mohamed Elaraby [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 18:36:59 UTC (666 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled SynClaimEval: A Framework for Evaluating the Utility of Synthetic Data in Long-Context Claim Verification, by Mohamed Elaraby and Jyoti Prakash Maheswari\nL34: \nL35:   * cite7†View PDF L36:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite13†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite14†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L45: \nL46: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite20†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.or