ogress has been made in spoken question answering (SQA) in recent years. However, many existing methods, including large audio language models, struggle with processing long audio. Follow the success of retrieval augmented generation, a speech-related retriever shows promising in help preprocessing long-form speech. But the performance of existing speech-related retrievers is lacking.\nL17: To address this challenge, we propose CLSR, an end-to-end contrastive language-speech retriever that efficiently extracts question-relevant segments from long audio recordings for downstream SQA task. Unlike conventional speech-text contrastive models, CLSR incorporates an intermediate step that converts acoustic features into text-like representations prior to alignment, thereby more effectively bridging the gap between modalities.\nL18: Experimental results across four cross-modal retrieval datasets demonstrate that CLSR surpasses both end-to-end speech related retrievers and pipeline approaches combining speech recognition with text retrieval, providing a robust foundation for advancing practical long-form SQA applications.\nL19: Comments:  | 12 pages, 7 figures, accepted by AAAI 2026\nL20: Subjects:  | Sound (cs.SD); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.09282 [cs.SD]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.09282v1 [cs.SD] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09282†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jiliang Hu [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 12:49:30 UTC (1,115 KB)\nL28: cite17†[v2] Wed, 21 Jan 2026 07:47:47 UTC (1,315 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v3] Sat, 11 Apr 2026 11:33:33 UTC (1,283 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled End-to-end Contrastive Language-Speech Pretraining Model For Long-form Spoken Question Answering, by Jiliang Hu and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite